A||N||1||rnoswal||Super duty block and crankshaft||||1||
Z||000000||rnoswal||12-12-2000||12:23 AM||||I wish people would stop saying that the only way this engine will survive any real power is to go with a superduty block and crank! Who can afford this stuff anyway! Ford, Chrysler, and Toyota already have great block and crank combinations that will easily handle 200 horsepower and 7000 rpms and they are all over the junk yards, I don't mean because of the horsepower either!! I own an 85 Fiero and really like it. It has the 2.5 in it and I really hate the fact that the crank is so weak. I don't need to turn 10,000 rpms, but I would like to put a cam in it that will make it come alive some. I also can't believe that no one has tried putting on a carb that will breath better than the current efi system. What is the most anyone has done to there Iron Duke and still drives it on the street and having fun?<P>Russ||||reg||1
Z||000001||WKDFIRO||12-12-2000||07:23 AM||||Carbing it here in Sunny, Smoggy, Shakey, Southern California is an impossibility if we want to keep it smogged, (who REALLY wants to keep going back to EFI every two years for the smog check?)<P>As for breathing, I ripped out that sorry POS exhaust manifold and replaced it with the Hooker Super Comp Tri-Y as well as taking out the air box and catalytic convertor (at least I could easily put it back on since the straight pipe was flanged.) I also used a V6 clutch (I got a deal on it) Back in the day the WLDFIRO (87 coupe 5 spd) could almost keep up with the GTs of my club AND still get great gas mileage!<P>The crank can be upgraded with the stronger S-10 Iron Duke Crankshaft and the 87-88 Iron Dukes had roller cam lifters. I think I have seen one picture of someone years ago who had created their own Multi-port Fuel injection for the 4 cylinder Fiero. I saw the picture at FOCOA headquarter during a visit and it was a really nice set up.||||reg||9
